1
00:00:00,500 --> 00:00:04,451
A seventy billion parameter model is a hundred and forty gigabytes of

2
00:00:04,451 --> 00:00:08,239
weights. So training it should need a hundred and forty gigabytes,

3
00:00:08,239 --> 00:00:09,159
right? Wrong.

4
00:00:09,159 --> 00:00:10,891
Training needs one thousand,

5
00:00:10,891 --> 00:00:12,894
one hundred and twenty gigabytes,

6
00:00:12,894 --> 00:00:14,031
eight times more.

7
00:00:14,381 --> 00:00:15,946
No single GPU holds that.

8
00:00:15,946 --> 00:00:18,375
So how do people train big models at all?

9
00:00:18,375 --> 00:00:19,833
This lesson answers it.

10
00:00:19,833 --> 00:00:21,237
Where the memory goes,

11
00:00:21,237 --> 00:00:22,316
how to split it,

12
00:00:22,316 --> 00:00:23,288
what it costs,

13
00:00:23,288 --> 00:00:25,123
and what breaks along the way.

14
00:00:26,373 --> 00:00:29,809
Welcome to lesson five of the cloud for A I engineers course,

15
00:00:29,809 --> 00:00:31,131
on The Code Notebook.

16
00:00:31,481 --> 00:00:34,615
The handwritten notes and the code for this lesson are linked in

17
00:00:34,615 --> 00:00:35,537
the description.

18
00:00:36,787 --> 00:00:39,223
In the earlier lessons we rented chips,

19
00:00:39,223 --> 00:00:41,489
packed containers and fed them data.

20
00:00:41,489 --> 00:00:42,453
Now we train.

21
00:00:42,453 --> 00:00:44,946
This is the most expensive thing in A I,

22
00:00:44,946 --> 00:00:46,759
so it pays to understand it.

23
00:00:47,109 --> 00:00:48,535
Plan for the lesson.

24
00:00:48,535 --> 00:00:49,842
The memory ledger,

25
00:00:49,842 --> 00:00:51,744
how to share it across GPUs,

26
00:00:51,744 --> 00:00:54,003
how to save memory by recomputing,

27
00:00:54,003 --> 00:00:56,083
how many GPU hours a run needs,

28
00:00:56,083 --> 00:00:57,866
the ways to split a model,

29
00:00:57,866 --> 00:00:58,817
the network,

30
00:00:58,817 --> 00:01:00,243
and what goes wrong.

31
00:01:00,243 --> 00:01:03,452
Some of it is measured on a small model on my CPU.

32
00:01:03,452 --> 00:01:05,948
The rest is arithmetic and simulation,

33
00:01:05,948 --> 00:01:06,721
labelled.

34
00:01:07,971 --> 00:01:09,414
First, what is training?

35
00:01:09,414 --> 00:01:11,476
Think of a teacher marking homework.

36
00:01:11,476 --> 00:01:12,919
The model makes a guess.

37
00:01:12,919 --> 00:01:14,414
That is the forward pass.

38
00:01:14,414 --> 00:01:16,064
We measure how wrong it was.

39
00:01:16,064 --> 00:01:17,868
That number is called the loss.

40
00:01:18,218 --> 00:01:20,093
Then comes the backward pass.

41
00:01:20,093 --> 00:01:23,842
We work out which numbers inside the model were most to blame.

42
00:01:23,842 --> 00:01:26,341
Those blame scores are called gradients.

43
00:01:26,341 --> 00:01:29,693
And finally the optimiser nudges every weight a little,

44
00:01:29,693 --> 00:01:32,193
in the direction that reduces the error.

45
00:01:32,193 --> 00:01:33,840
Repeat billions of times.

46
00:01:35,090 --> 00:01:36,166
During training,

47
00:01:36,166 --> 00:01:37,781
four things sit in memory.

48
00:01:37,781 --> 00:01:39,234
The weights themselves.

49
00:01:39,234 --> 00:01:40,202
The gradients,

50
00:01:40,202 --> 00:01:41,224
one per weight.

51
00:01:41,224 --> 00:01:42,677
The optimiser's memory,

52
00:01:42,677 --> 00:01:44,453
and I will explain that next.

53
00:01:44,453 --> 00:01:45,529
And activations,

54
00:01:45,529 --> 00:01:49,403
the in between results saved during the forward pass so the backward

55
00:01:49,403 --> 00:01:50,587
pass can use them.

56
00:01:50,937 --> 00:01:53,869
The first three scale with the number of parameters.

57
00:01:53,869 --> 00:01:56,852
Activations scale with how many tokens are in flight.

58
00:01:56,852 --> 00:02:00,569
Together they are why training needs so much more than just storing

59
00:02:00,569 --> 00:02:01,302
the model.

60
00:02:02,552 --> 00:02:05,053
Here is the ledger for the standard recipe.

61
00:02:05,053 --> 00:02:06,969
Weights in the half size format,

62
00:02:06,969 --> 00:02:07,714
two bytes.

63
00:02:07,714 --> 00:02:09,044
Gradients, two bytes.

64
00:02:09,044 --> 00:02:10,268
Then the optimiser.

65
00:02:10,268 --> 00:02:11,226
Most use Adam,

66
00:02:11,226 --> 00:02:13,301
which keeps two numbers per weight,

67
00:02:13,301 --> 00:02:15,323
a running average of the gradient,

68
00:02:15,323 --> 00:02:16,494
and of its square,

69
00:02:16,494 --> 00:02:17,558
four bytes each.

70
00:02:17,908 --> 00:02:21,771
Adam also keeps a full precision master copy of the weights,

71
00:02:21,771 --> 00:02:22,676
four bytes,

72
00:02:22,676 --> 00:02:25,634
because tiny nudges vanish in half precision.

73
00:02:25,634 --> 00:02:29,255
Two plus two plus twelve is sixteen bytes per parameter.

74
00:02:29,605 --> 00:02:30,254
The weights,

75
00:02:30,254 --> 00:02:31,511
the part you thought about,

76
00:02:31,511 --> 00:02:32,890
are only a eighth of the bill.

77
00:02:34,140 --> 00:02:36,157
Let me check that on a real model.

78
00:02:36,157 --> 00:02:40,191
I built a small network with thirty-three and a half million parameters,

79
00:02:40,191 --> 00:02:42,102
ran one training step with Adam,

80
00:02:42,102 --> 00:02:44,385
and added up the bytes of every tensor.

81
00:02:44,735 --> 00:02:47,256
On a CPU everything is full precision,

82
00:02:47,256 --> 00:02:49,056
so weights are four bytes,

83
00:02:49,056 --> 00:02:50,197
gradients four,

84
00:02:50,197 --> 00:02:52,238
and Adam's two memories eight.

85
00:02:52,238 --> 00:02:55,239
Total, sixteen point zero bytes per parameter,

86
00:02:55,239 --> 00:02:57,040
exactly. Different recipe,

87
00:02:57,040 --> 00:02:57,940
same total.

88
00:02:59,190 --> 00:03:00,173
Now scale it.

89
00:03:00,173 --> 00:03:04,975
Sixteen bytes times seven billion parameters is a hundred and twelve gigabytes.

90
00:03:04,975 --> 00:03:06,132
Seventy billion,

91
00:03:06,132 --> 00:03:07,868
eleven hundred and twenty.

92
00:03:07,868 --> 00:03:09,835
Four hundred and five billion,

93
00:03:09,835 --> 00:03:11,917
nearly six and a half terabytes.

94
00:03:12,267 --> 00:03:16,311
An eighty gigabyte H one hundred cannot hold even the seven billion

95
00:03:16,311 --> 00:03:17,848
model's training state.

96
00:03:17,848 --> 00:03:19,158
Before activations,

97
00:03:19,158 --> 00:03:20,582
before anything else.

98
00:03:20,582 --> 00:03:21,664
So we share it.

99
00:03:22,914 --> 00:03:25,171
The trick is called sharding,

100
00:03:25,171 --> 00:03:27,361
with names ZeRO and F S D P.

101
00:03:27,361 --> 00:03:30,371
Instead of every GPU keeping everything,

102
00:03:30,371 --> 00:03:32,218
each GPU keeps a slice.

103
00:03:32,218 --> 00:03:35,844
The first stage shards only the optimiser memory.

104
00:03:35,844 --> 00:03:38,512
The next also shards the gradients.

105
00:03:38,862 --> 00:03:41,416
The last stage shards the weights too.

106
00:03:41,416 --> 00:03:44,516
For a seventy billion model on sixty-four GPUs,

107
00:03:44,516 --> 00:03:48,711
the per GPU share falls from eleven hundred and twenty gigabytes,

108
00:03:48,711 --> 00:03:50,170
to two ninety-three,

109
00:03:50,170 --> 00:03:52,115
to a hundred and fifty-five,

110
00:03:52,115 --> 00:03:54,547
and finally to seventeen and a half.

111
00:03:54,897 --> 00:03:57,609
That last number fits an eighty gigabyte card.

112
00:03:57,609 --> 00:03:59,561
The earlier stages still do not.

113
00:04:00,811 --> 00:04:04,152
Why do the first two stages not work for seventy billion?

114
00:04:04,152 --> 00:04:07,383
They still keep a full copy of the half size weights on

115
00:04:07,383 --> 00:04:08,150
every GPU.

116
00:04:08,150 --> 00:04:11,600
That is a hundred and forty gigabytes before anything else.

117
00:04:11,950 --> 00:04:13,953
Only the full sharding stage,

118
00:04:13,953 --> 00:04:15,955
called ZeRO three or F S D P,

119
00:04:15,955 --> 00:04:17,837
splits the weights as well.

120
00:04:17,837 --> 00:04:20,143
The price is more network traffic,

121
00:04:20,143 --> 00:04:23,602
about one and a half times a plain data parallel run,

122
00:04:23,602 --> 00:04:26,819
because the weights must be gathered when needed.

123
00:04:28,069 --> 00:04:29,384
Now the fourth thing,

124
00:04:29,384 --> 00:04:31,541
activations. During the forward pass,

125
00:04:31,541 --> 00:04:34,014
every layer's in between results are saved,

126
00:04:34,014 --> 00:04:36,171
because the backward pass needs them.

127
00:04:36,171 --> 00:04:38,276
They grow with the number of tokens,

128
00:04:38,276 --> 00:04:39,486
and with attention,

129
00:04:39,486 --> 00:04:42,485
they can grow with the square of the sequence length.

130
00:04:42,835 --> 00:04:45,725
That is why long context training is so hungry.

131
00:04:45,725 --> 00:04:50,032
The notebook's worked example for a seventy billion class model at eight

132
00:04:50,032 --> 00:04:54,283
thousand tokens is about one point nine terabytes of activations if you

133
00:04:54,283 --> 00:04:55,416
keep everything,

134
00:04:55,416 --> 00:04:59,950
against about twenty-three gigabytes with recomputation and tensor parallel.

135
00:04:59,950 --> 00:05:04,030
A technique called flash attention avoids storing the biggest piece,

136
00:05:04,030 --> 00:05:07,260
and the standard trick for the rest is recomputation.

137
00:05:08,510 --> 00:05:11,771
Recomputation, also called gradient checkpointing,

138
00:05:11,771 --> 00:05:12,676
is a trade.

139
00:05:12,676 --> 00:05:14,669
Keep only each block's input.

140
00:05:14,669 --> 00:05:17,990
When the backward pass needs the inside of a block,

141
00:05:17,990 --> 00:05:20,405
run that block's forward pass again.

142
00:05:20,755 --> 00:05:23,227
You spend extra compute to save memory.

143
00:05:23,227 --> 00:05:24,262
I measured it.

144
00:05:24,262 --> 00:05:25,297
Twelve blocks,

145
00:05:25,297 --> 00:05:26,677
two thousand tokens,

146
00:05:26,677 --> 00:05:27,942
on one CPU thread.

147
00:05:27,942 --> 00:05:30,242
Each setting ran in its own process,

148
00:05:30,242 --> 00:05:32,312
so I could read its peak memory.

149
00:05:32,312 --> 00:05:33,864
The machine was shared,

150
00:05:33,864 --> 00:05:35,302
so timings are noisy.

151
00:05:36,552 --> 00:05:37,816
Keeping everything,

152
00:05:37,816 --> 00:05:41,443
the activations took seven hundred and ninety-three megabytes.

153
00:05:41,443 --> 00:05:42,707
With recomputation,

154
00:05:42,707 --> 00:05:44,466
four hundred and twenty-six.

155
00:05:44,466 --> 00:05:45,290
About half.

156
00:05:45,640 --> 00:05:49,219
The step time rose from five point six seconds to six point

157
00:05:49,219 --> 00:05:51,492
nine. A little under a quarter more.

158
00:05:51,842 --> 00:05:55,599
Half the activation memory for a fifth to a quarter more time.

159
00:05:55,599 --> 00:05:59,185
The notebook's rule of thumb is up to a third more compute,

160
00:05:59,185 --> 00:06:00,551
so mine is in range.

161
00:06:01,801 --> 00:06:02,702
Now compute.

162
00:06:02,702 --> 00:06:03,604
A good rule:

163
00:06:03,604 --> 00:06:07,886
training a model needs about six times the parameters times the training

164
00:06:07,886 --> 00:06:10,139
tokens in floating point operations.

165
00:06:10,139 --> 00:06:11,773
Two for the forward pass,

166
00:06:11,773 --> 00:06:13,238
four for the backward,

167
00:06:13,238 --> 00:06:14,815
per parameter per token.

168
00:06:15,165 --> 00:06:19,599
Those operations run on GPUs at a fraction of their peak speed.

169
00:06:19,599 --> 00:06:21,783
The fraction is called M F U,

170
00:06:21,783 --> 00:06:23,637
model flops utilisation.

171
00:06:23,637 --> 00:06:28,402
Well tuned dense runs on H one hundreds reach roughly thirty-five to

172
00:06:28,402 --> 00:06:29,924
forty-five percent.

173
00:06:31,174 --> 00:06:33,091
Let us do a worked example.

174
00:06:33,091 --> 00:06:36,617
A seven billion model trained on two trillion tokens.

175
00:06:36,617 --> 00:06:41,071
Six times seven billion times two trillion is eight point four times

176
00:06:41,071 --> 00:06:43,360
ten to the twenty-two operations.

177
00:06:43,710 --> 00:06:48,941
An H one hundred peaks near nine hundred and eighty-nine trillion operations

178
00:06:48,941 --> 00:06:49,791
a second.

179
00:06:49,791 --> 00:06:51,556
At forty percent M F U,

180
00:06:51,556 --> 00:06:53,583
that is about four hundred.

181
00:06:53,583 --> 00:06:57,899
Divide, and the run needs about fifty-nine thousand GPU hours.

182
00:06:57,899 --> 00:07:00,449
On a thousand and twenty-four GPUs,

183
00:07:00,449 --> 00:07:02,018
two point four days.

184
00:07:02,368 --> 00:07:04,916
At an assumed two dollars per GPU hour,

185
00:07:04,916 --> 00:07:07,879
about a hundred and eighteen thousand dollars.

186
00:07:07,879 --> 00:07:09,478
Real prices vary a lot.

187
00:07:10,728 --> 00:07:12,420
One warning about M F U.

188
00:07:12,420 --> 00:07:15,018
It depends on which peak you divide by.

189
00:07:15,018 --> 00:07:18,824
Run in a smaller number format and the chip's peak doubles,

190
00:07:18,824 --> 00:07:21,482
so the same run looks half as efficient.

191
00:07:21,482 --> 00:07:25,107
Compare M F U only between runs that use the same number

192
00:07:25,107 --> 00:07:25,771
format.

193
00:07:26,121 --> 00:07:27,400
Also quote M F U,

194
00:07:27,400 --> 00:07:28,253
not H F U.

195
00:07:28,253 --> 00:07:30,567
The second counts recomputed work,

196
00:07:30,567 --> 00:07:34,830
so recomputation makes it look better while the useful work is the

197
00:07:34,830 --> 00:07:36,596
same. As a reverse check,

198
00:07:36,596 --> 00:07:41,163
DeepSeek reported about two point eight million GPU hours for its large

199
00:07:41,163 --> 00:07:45,122
model, which works out to roughly a third of the chip's peak.

200
00:07:46,372 --> 00:07:48,617
How many tokens should you train on?

201
00:07:48,617 --> 00:07:53,333
A famous study called Chinchilla suggested about twenty tokens per parameter for

202
00:07:53,333 --> 00:07:55,747
the best use of a fixed compute budget.

203
00:07:56,097 --> 00:07:59,085
But today's models train far longer on purpose.

204
00:07:59,085 --> 00:08:03,536
Llama three's biggest model saw about thirty-eight tokens per parameter,

205
00:08:03,536 --> 00:08:07,637
and a seven billion model on two trillion tokens sees nearly three

206
00:08:07,637 --> 00:08:08,984
hundred. A smaller,

207
00:08:08,984 --> 00:08:12,147
longer trained model is cheaper to run afterwards.

208
00:08:13,397 --> 00:08:16,385
Sharding the state is one way to share the work.

209
00:08:16,385 --> 00:08:19,430
There are four main ways to split a training job.

210
00:08:19,430 --> 00:08:20,464
Data parallel,

211
00:08:20,464 --> 00:08:23,796
where every GPU runs the same model on different data.

212
00:08:23,796 --> 00:08:24,945
Tensor parallel,

213
00:08:24,945 --> 00:08:27,933
where the matrices inside each layer are cut up.

214
00:08:28,283 --> 00:08:29,526
Pipeline parallel,

215
00:08:29,526 --> 00:08:31,728
where layers are split into stages,

216
00:08:31,728 --> 00:08:33,197
like an assembly line.

217
00:08:33,197 --> 00:08:34,552
And expert parallel,

218
00:08:34,552 --> 00:08:36,586
for models made of many experts.

219
00:08:36,586 --> 00:08:39,014
Big runs combine three or four at once.

220
00:08:39,364 --> 00:08:41,087
Each one cuts a different thing,

221
00:08:41,087 --> 00:08:43,816
so each one sends different traffic over the network.

222
00:08:45,066 --> 00:08:47,110
Tensor parallel is the chattiest.

223
00:08:47,110 --> 00:08:47,994
Every layer,

224
00:08:47,994 --> 00:08:49,375
forward and backward,

225
00:08:49,375 --> 00:08:52,027
the GPUs must combine their partial results,

226
00:08:52,027 --> 00:08:53,961
and the next step waits for it.

227
00:08:53,961 --> 00:08:56,336
That traffic sits on the critical path.

228
00:08:56,686 --> 00:09:00,107
So tensor parallel belongs only on the fastest link,

229
00:09:00,107 --> 00:09:02,428
the vendor link inside one server.

230
00:09:02,428 --> 00:09:06,521
That is why an eight GPU server usually caps tensor parallel at

231
00:09:06,521 --> 00:09:07,132
eight.

232
00:09:08,382 --> 00:09:11,499
Pipeline parallel splits the layers into stages,

233
00:09:11,499 --> 00:09:12,818
one stage per GPU,

234
00:09:12,818 --> 00:09:14,376
like an assembly line.

235
00:09:14,376 --> 00:09:17,613
It only sends the boundary results between stages,

236
00:09:17,613 --> 00:09:20,970
so it copes with the slower network between servers.

237
00:09:21,320 --> 00:09:22,854
The cost is the bubble.

238
00:09:22,854 --> 00:09:25,582
At the start the later stages wait for work,

239
00:09:25,582 --> 00:09:28,139
and at the end the early stages sit idle.

240
00:09:28,139 --> 00:09:29,162
To measure it,

241
00:09:29,162 --> 00:09:32,345
I simulated the schedule and counted the idle slots.

242
00:09:33,595 --> 00:09:36,927
Here is a four stage pipeline with four micro batches.

243
00:09:36,927 --> 00:09:38,479
Blue is a forward step,

244
00:09:38,479 --> 00:09:40,203
orange is a backward step.

245
00:09:40,553 --> 00:09:42,611
Gray is a GPU doing nothing.

246
00:09:42,611 --> 00:09:44,670
Six of every fourteen slots,

247
00:09:44,670 --> 00:09:47,114
forty-two point nine percent idle,

248
00:09:47,114 --> 00:09:49,623
and it matches the formula exactly.

249
00:09:49,623 --> 00:09:51,874
The bubble is stages minus one,

250
00:09:51,874 --> 00:09:54,769
over micro batches plus stages minus one.

251
00:09:56,019 --> 00:09:57,739
More stages make it worse.

252
00:09:57,739 --> 00:10:01,697
Eight stages with eight micro batches wastes forty-seven percent.

253
00:10:01,697 --> 00:10:04,049
Give the pipeline more micro batches,

254
00:10:04,049 --> 00:10:06,859
thirty-two, and it drops to eighteen percent.

255
00:10:06,859 --> 00:10:08,637
A hundred and twenty-eight,

256
00:10:08,637 --> 00:10:09,612
five percent.

257
00:10:09,962 --> 00:10:14,503
A trick called interleaving gives each GPU several smaller chunks and cuts

258
00:10:14,503 --> 00:10:16,948
the bubble to roughly a quarter again.

259
00:10:16,948 --> 00:10:18,404
The lesson is simple.

260
00:10:18,404 --> 00:10:19,976
Keep the pipeline full.

261
00:10:21,226 --> 00:10:22,980
So where does each split go?

262
00:10:22,980 --> 00:10:25,721
The rule is that the more often a group talks,

263
00:10:25,721 --> 00:10:27,530
the faster the link it needs.

264
00:10:27,530 --> 00:10:30,928
Tensor parallel goes inside one server on the vendor link.

265
00:10:30,928 --> 00:10:32,737
Pipeline goes across servers.

266
00:10:32,737 --> 00:10:33,724
Data parallel,

267
00:10:33,724 --> 00:10:35,478
which overlaps with compute,

268
00:10:35,478 --> 00:10:36,519
goes outermost.

269
00:10:36,869 --> 00:10:41,694
Llama three's biggest model was reported as tensor parallel eight inside each

270
00:10:41,694 --> 00:10:43,422
server, pipeline sixteen,

271
00:10:43,422 --> 00:10:46,698
and data parallel up to a hundred and twenty-eight.

272
00:10:46,698 --> 00:10:47,830
A memory trick:

273
00:10:47,830 --> 00:10:49,617
tiny cats eat pizza daily,

274
00:10:49,617 --> 00:10:50,510
for tensor,

275
00:10:50,510 --> 00:10:51,701
context, expert,

276
00:10:51,701 --> 00:10:52,833
pipeline, data.

277
00:10:54,083 --> 00:10:55,293
Now the network,

278
00:10:55,293 --> 00:10:56,200
in numbers.

279
00:10:56,200 --> 00:11:01,099
Gradients for a seven billion model in half precision are fourteen gigabytes.

280
00:11:01,099 --> 00:11:04,305
A ring all reduce sends about twice that per GPU,

281
00:11:04,305 --> 00:11:05,938
twenty-eight gigabytes.

282
00:11:06,288 --> 00:11:09,173
At fifty gigabytes per second between servers,

283
00:11:09,173 --> 00:11:12,867
that is point five six seconds per step if nothing overlaps.

284
00:11:12,867 --> 00:11:15,695
On the vendor link at four hundred and fifty,

285
00:11:15,695 --> 00:11:16,676
point oh six.

286
00:11:16,676 --> 00:11:20,370
A seventy billion model over the slow link is five point six

287
00:11:20,370 --> 00:11:21,582
seconds per step.

288
00:11:21,582 --> 00:11:24,237
This is arithmetic from vendor bandwidths.

289
00:11:25,487 --> 00:11:26,655
A nasty gotcha.

290
00:11:26,655 --> 00:11:30,834
The library that does these group communications is called N C C

291
00:11:30,834 --> 00:11:34,459
L. If the right network plugin or settings are missing,

292
00:11:34,459 --> 00:11:37,163
it quietly falls back to a slower route.

293
00:11:37,163 --> 00:11:38,393
Nothing crashes.

294
00:11:38,393 --> 00:11:41,219
Your job is just two to five times slower.

295
00:11:41,569 --> 00:11:44,411
The habit to build is to turn on N C C L

296
00:11:44,411 --> 00:11:46,930
debug logging on every new cluster,

297
00:11:46,930 --> 00:11:49,449
and read which transport it picked.

298
00:11:49,449 --> 00:11:53,260
Ten seconds of reading can save a month of wasted GPUs.

299
00:11:54,510 --> 00:11:55,995
Number formats again.

300
00:11:55,995 --> 00:11:58,610
B F sixteen is the default for training.

301
00:11:58,610 --> 00:12:01,581
Eight bit floats are now proven in production,

302
00:12:01,581 --> 00:12:04,553
DeepSeek trained a very large model with them.

303
00:12:04,553 --> 00:12:08,415
And the newest four bit format matched eight bit quality on a

304
00:12:08,415 --> 00:12:12,218
twelve billion model over ten trillion tokens in one report.

305
00:12:12,568 --> 00:12:17,018
Smaller formats mean less memory and faster maths on chips that support

306
00:12:17,018 --> 00:12:19,450
them. But each needs careful recipes,

307
00:12:19,450 --> 00:12:21,646
so adopt them one step at a time.

308
00:12:22,896 --> 00:12:23,656
At scale,

309
00:12:23,656 --> 00:12:25,412
things break all the time.

310
00:12:25,412 --> 00:12:29,859
Llama three's training run reported four hundred and nineteen unexpected

311
00:12:29,859 --> 00:12:32,024
interruptions in fifty-four days.

312
00:12:32,024 --> 00:12:34,598
That is one every three point one hours.

313
00:12:34,948 --> 00:12:35,828
Plan for it.

314
00:12:35,828 --> 00:12:37,752
Save checkpoints on a schedule,

315
00:12:37,752 --> 00:12:39,895
like lesson two's square root rule.

316
00:12:39,895 --> 00:12:42,424
Save them asynchronously like lesson four.

317
00:12:42,424 --> 00:12:45,173
And restart automatically from the latest one.

318
00:12:46,423 --> 00:12:48,182
Most teams never pretrain.

319
00:12:48,182 --> 00:12:50,351
They fine tune an existing model.

320
00:12:50,351 --> 00:12:52,521
And the cheap way is called LoRA.

321
00:12:52,521 --> 00:12:56,508
Freeze the whole model and train two small matrices next to each

322
00:12:56,508 --> 00:12:57,564
layer instead.

323
00:12:57,914 --> 00:12:59,969
I measured it on my small model.

324
00:12:59,969 --> 00:13:01,112
With rank eight,

325
00:13:01,112 --> 00:13:04,138
LoRA trained under one percent of the parameters,

326
00:13:04,138 --> 00:13:08,821
and the optimiser's memory shrank from two hundred and sixty-nine megabytes to

327
00:13:08,821 --> 00:13:09,849
two point six.

328
00:13:09,849 --> 00:13:11,276
A hundred times less.

329
00:13:12,526 --> 00:13:15,414
The usual fine tuning order has three steps.

330
00:13:15,414 --> 00:13:19,144
First, supervised fine tuning on examples of good answers.

331
00:13:19,144 --> 00:13:20,888
Then preference training,

332
00:13:20,888 --> 00:13:21,971
such as D P O,

333
00:13:21,971 --> 00:13:25,700
where the model learns which of two answers people prefer.

334
00:13:25,700 --> 00:13:27,204
Then, for some tasks,

335
00:13:27,204 --> 00:13:29,911
reinforcement learning against a checker.

336
00:13:30,261 --> 00:13:34,053
A variant called Q LoRA also stores the frozen weights in four

337
00:13:34,053 --> 00:13:36,466
bits, so a big model fits on one card.

338
00:13:36,466 --> 00:13:37,960
I am describing these.

339
00:13:37,960 --> 00:13:40,316
They are not measured in this lesson.

340
00:13:41,566 --> 00:13:43,456
A quick map of the tools.

341
00:13:43,456 --> 00:13:45,020
PyTorch's own stack,

342
00:13:45,020 --> 00:13:46,846
with torchrun to launch,

343
00:13:46,846 --> 00:13:48,475
F S D P two to shard,

344
00:13:48,475 --> 00:13:50,691
and torchtitan as a reference.

345
00:13:50,691 --> 00:13:55,058
NVIDIA's Megatron for thousand GPU runs and mixture of experts.

346
00:13:55,058 --> 00:13:56,949
JAX with MaxText on TPUs.

347
00:13:56,949 --> 00:14:01,446
And Hugging Face Trainer with DeepSpeed for one machine to a few,

348
00:14:01,446 --> 00:14:03,010
and for fine tuning.

349
00:14:03,360 --> 00:14:04,870
For most people reading this,

350
00:14:04,870 --> 00:14:06,516
the last one is the right start.

351
00:14:07,766 --> 00:14:09,143
For ShopNest Copilot,

352
00:14:09,143 --> 00:14:10,520
we will not pretrain.

353
00:14:10,520 --> 00:14:13,879
We will fine tune an eight billion model to answer in our

354
00:14:13,879 --> 00:14:14,870
store's style.

355
00:14:14,870 --> 00:14:19,110
Full fine tuning would need about a hundred and twenty-eight gigabytes of

356
00:14:19,110 --> 00:14:20,157
training state.

357
00:14:20,157 --> 00:14:22,249
Sixteen bytes times eight billion.

358
00:14:22,599 --> 00:14:26,559
LoRA keeps the frozen weights at sixteen gigabytes in the half size

359
00:14:26,559 --> 00:14:29,068
format, plus a few megabytes of adapters,

360
00:14:29,068 --> 00:14:30,462
and it fits one card.

361
00:14:30,462 --> 00:14:31,633
That is our plan.

362
00:14:32,883 --> 00:14:34,865
Four words to carry forward.

363
00:14:34,865 --> 00:14:36,104
Optimiser state:

364
00:14:36,104 --> 00:14:39,262
the memory the optimiser keeps for each weight.

365
00:14:39,262 --> 00:14:42,730
Sharding: giving each GPU a slice instead of a copy.

366
00:14:43,080 --> 00:14:46,097
Recompute: trading extra compute for less memory.

367
00:14:46,097 --> 00:14:46,951
And bubble:

368
00:14:46,951 --> 00:14:49,968
the idle time at the start and end of a pipeline.

369
00:14:51,218 --> 00:14:52,437
Interview corner.

370
00:14:52,437 --> 00:14:55,630
Asked how much memory training needs per parameter,

371
00:14:55,630 --> 00:14:58,997
say: about sixteen bytes with Adam in mixed precision.

372
00:14:58,997 --> 00:15:00,158
Two for weights,

373
00:15:00,158 --> 00:15:01,435
two for gradients,

374
00:15:01,435 --> 00:15:04,629
twelve for the master copy and Adam's two memories.

375
00:15:04,629 --> 00:15:07,531
I measured sixteen point zero on a real model.

376
00:15:07,881 --> 00:15:10,056
Asked how to fit a big model,

377
00:15:10,056 --> 00:15:13,484
say: shard the state with ZeRO three or F S D P,

378
00:15:13,484 --> 00:15:15,197
recompute activations,

379
00:15:15,197 --> 00:15:20,404
and split with tensor parallel inside a server and pipeline across servers.

380
00:15:21,654 --> 00:15:22,477
Your turn.

381
00:15:22,477 --> 00:15:25,123
The zip for this lesson has four scripts.

382
00:15:25,123 --> 00:15:27,239
The memory ledger and LoRA test,

383
00:15:27,239 --> 00:15:28,827
the recomputation test,

384
00:15:28,827 --> 00:15:30,826
the pipeline bubble simulator,

385
00:15:30,826 --> 00:15:32,766
and the GPU hours calculator.

386
00:15:33,116 --> 00:15:36,675
Change the model size or the number of micro batches and see

387
00:15:36,675 --> 00:15:37,509
what moves.

388
00:15:37,509 --> 00:15:40,845
One changed number teaches more than an hour of reading.

389
00:15:42,095 --> 00:15:45,400
Recap. Training needs sixteen bytes per parameter,

390
00:15:45,400 --> 00:15:46,135
not two,

391
00:15:46,135 --> 00:15:50,603
so a seventy billion model needs eleven hundred and twenty gigabytes.

392
00:15:50,603 --> 00:15:54,275
Sharding gets it down to seventeen and a half per GPU on

393
00:15:54,275 --> 00:15:59,110
sixty-four. Recompute halves activation memory for about a fifth more time.

394
00:15:59,460 --> 00:16:04,074
A seven billion run on two trillion tokens is about fifty-nine thousand

395
00:16:04,074 --> 00:16:04,936
GPU hours.

396
00:16:04,936 --> 00:16:07,950
Keep the chattiest split on the fastest link.

397
00:16:07,950 --> 00:16:10,042
LoRA trains under one percent.

398
00:16:10,042 --> 00:16:11,027
